from unit_tests import U

U('Working scientifically',
 [('Which piece of equipment should you use to measure 50 cm³ of water accurately?','Measuring cylinder.','Beaker.','Conical flask.','A measuring cylinder has a fine scale for volume.'),
  ('A balance reads 0.3 g with nothing on it. This causes…','a systematic error.','a random error.','an anomaly.','Every reading will be 0.3 g too high.'),
  ('Which graph should you draw for temperature against time?','A line graph.','A bar chart.','A pie chart.','Both variables are continuous.')],
 [('Name the independent and dependent variables when testing how the length of a pendulum affects the time for one swing.','Independent: length of the pendulum.~Dependent: time for one swing.'),
  ('Times for ten swings were 14.2 s, 14.6 s and 14.4 s. Calculate the mean time for one swing.','Mean for ten swings = (14.2 + 14.6 + 14.4) ÷ 3 = 14.4 s.~One swing = 14.4 ÷ 10…~… = 1.44 s.'),
  ('Explain why scientists repeat measurements and calculate a mean.','Readings vary because of random errors.~Repeats help you spot anomalies.~A mean of repeats is closer to the true value / more reliable.'),
  ('A student heats water with a Bunsen burner. Describe four things she should do to work safely.','Wear safety goggles.~Use a heatproof mat and the yellow flame when not heating.~Tie back long hair and stand up.~Use tongs or wait for equipment to cool before touching it.')])

U('Cells',
 [('Which cell part controls what enters and leaves the cell?','Cell membrane.','Cell wall.','Nucleus.','The membrane controls movement in and out.'),
  ('An eyepiece is ×10 and the objective is ×10. The total magnification is…','×100.','×20.','×1.','10 × 10 = 100.'),
  ('Oxygen moves into cells by…','diffusion.','filtration.','condensation.','From high to low concentration.')],
 [('Name two parts found in plant cells but not in animal cells.','Cell wall / chloroplast / permanent vacuole.~Any second correct part.'),
  ('Describe how to prepare a slide of cheek cells to see them clearly.','Gently scrape the inside of the cheek with a clean cotton bud and smear onto a slide.~Add a drop of stain such as methylene blue.~Lower a cover slip carefully to avoid air bubbles.'),
  ('Explain how a red blood cell is adapted to carry oxygen.','It has no nucleus, so there is more room for haemoglobin.~It has a biconcave (dimpled disc) shape giving a large surface area.~It is small and flexible to squeeze through capillaries.'),
  ('A drop of ink is added to still water. Describe and explain what happens over time, and how the result would change in warmer water.','The ink spreads out through the water.~Its particles move from high to low concentration (diffusion).~Particles keep moving randomly until they are evenly spread.~In warmer water particles move faster, so it spreads faster.')])

U('Movement',
 [('A group of similar cells working together is…','a tissue.','an organ.','an organ system.','Tissues are made of similar cells.'),
  ('What joins a muscle to a bone?','A tendon.','A ligament.','Cartilage.','Tendons attach muscles to bones.'),
  ('When you bend your arm, the biceps…','contracts.','relaxes.','pushes the bone.','The biceps pulls the forearm up.')],
 [('Give two functions of the skeleton.','Support / protection of organs.~Movement / making blood cells.'),
  ('Describe what the biceps and triceps do when you straighten your arm.','The triceps contracts and pulls the forearm down.~The biceps relaxes.~They work as an antagonistic pair.'),
  ('Explain why a joint needs cartilage and synovial fluid.','Cartilage covers the ends of the bones.~It is smooth and absorbs shocks.~Synovial fluid lubricates the joint so bones move without rubbing.'),
  ('Explain why muscles must work in pairs, using the knee as an example.','Muscles can only pull when they contract; they cannot push.~One muscle (at the front of the thigh) contracts to straighten the knee.~Another muscle (at the back of the thigh) contracts to bend it.~While one contracts, the other relaxes.')])

U('Particle model',
 [('In which state are particles far apart and moving quickly in all directions?','Gas.','Solid.','Liquid.','Gas particles are widely spaced.'),
  ('A liquid turning into a gas at its surface below the boiling point is…','evaporation.','condensation.','melting.','Evaporation happens at the surface.'),
  ('Heating a sealed container of gas makes the pressure…','increase.','decrease.','stay the same.','Particles hit the walls harder and more often.')],
 [('Describe the arrangement and movement of particles in a solid.','Particles are close together in a regular pattern.~They vibrate about fixed positions.'),
  ('Use the particle model to explain why a gas can be compressed but a liquid cannot.','In a gas there are large spaces between particles.~Pushing moves gas particles closer together.~In a liquid the particles are already touching, so there is no space.'),
  ('Explain the difference between an element, a compound and a mixture.','An element contains only one type of atom.~A compound has two or more elements chemically joined.~A mixture has substances that are not chemically joined and can be separated physically.'),
  ('Ice at −10 °C is heated until it becomes steam. Describe what happens to the particles at each stage.','The particles in the ice vibrate faster as it warms.~At 0 °C they break free of fixed positions and the ice melts.~In the liquid, particles move faster as it heats up.~At 100 °C they escape from the liquid completely as a gas (boiling).')])

U('Separating mixtures',
 [('Which method separates sand from water?','Filtration.','Distillation.','Chromatography.','Sand is insoluble, so filter paper traps it.'),
  ('In salty water, salt is the…','solute.','solvent.','solution.','The solute is what dissolves.'),
  ('Which method collects pure water from sea water?','Distillation.','Filtration.','Crystallisation.','Water evaporates and condenses; salt stays behind.')],
 [('10 g of sugar dissolves completely in 90 g of water. What is the mass of the solution? Give a reason.','100 g.~Mass is conserved when a substance dissolves.'),
  ('Describe how to obtain salt crystals from rock salt.','Dissolve the rock salt in warm water and stir.~Filter to remove the sand and grit.~Heat the filtrate to evaporate some water, then leave it to crystallise.'),
  ('A chromatogram of a brown sweet colouring shows three spots. A yellow colouring shows one spot at the same height as one of them. What can you conclude?','The brown colouring is a mixture of at least three dyes.~The yellow colouring is a single (pure) dye.~The brown colouring probably contains the yellow dye.'),
  ('Explain how distillation separates water from inky water.','The mixture is heated so the water boils/evaporates.~The ink does not evaporate and is left behind.~The water vapour passes into a condenser, where it is cooled.~It condenses back into pure liquid water, which is collected.')])

U('Energy costs',
 [('Which is a renewable energy resource?','Wind.','Coal.','Natural gas.','Wind will not run out.'),
  ('What is 2500 W in kilowatts?','2.5 kW.','25 kW.','0.25 kW.','Divide by 1000.'),
  ('A food sample raises water temperature more than another of the same mass. It probably…','contains more energy.','contains less energy.','has more water.','More energy was transferred to the water.')],
 [('Give one advantage and one disadvantage of using fossil fuels to generate electricity.','Advantage: reliable / available whenever needed.~Disadvantage: release carbon dioxide / will run out / pollution.'),
  ('A 2 kW heater is used for 3 hours. Electricity costs 28p per kWh. Calculate the cost.','Energy = 2 × 3 = 6 kWh.~Cost = 6 × 28…~… = 168p (£1.68).'),
  ('In the burning-food experiment, explain why the measured energy is less than the real energy in the food.','Some energy heats the air around the tube.~Some heats the tube and clamp, not the water.~Some food may not burn completely.'),
  ('Compare wind turbines and a coal power station for generating electricity.','Wind is renewable; coal will run out.~Wind does not release carbon dioxide when running; coal does.~Coal is reliable at any time; wind depends on the weather.~Wind farms take up lots of space / can be noisy or spoil views.')])

U('Energy transfers',
 [('A moving car has energy in its…','kinetic store.','elastic store.','nuclear store.','Moving objects have kinetic energy.'),
  ('A device transfers 200 J; 50 J is useful. Its efficiency is…','25%.','75%.','4%.','50 ÷ 200 × 100.'),
  ('Energy cannot be…','created or destroyed.','transferred.','stored.','The law of conservation of energy.')],
 [('Describe the energy transfer when a battery-powered fan is switched on.','Energy is transferred from the chemical store of the battery…~… to the kinetic store of the blades (and wasted to the thermal store of the surroundings).'),
  ('A kettle uses 2000 J and transfers 1800 J to the water. Calculate its efficiency as a percentage.','1800 ÷ 2000 = 0.9.~0.9 × 100…~… = 90%.'),
  ('Explain why an LED bulb is better than a filament bulb.','The LED is more efficient.~It wastes less energy by heating the surroundings.~So it uses less electricity for the same light, saving money.'),
  ('A ball is dropped from a height and bounces. Describe the energy changes until it comes to rest.','As it falls, energy transfers from the gravitational store to the kinetic store.~On impact some energy is stored elastically as it squashes.~Some energy is transferred to the thermal store of the ball and floor and carried away by sound.~Each bounce is lower until all the energy has spread to the surroundings.')])

U('Variation and adaptation',
 [('Which is an example of continuous variation?','Height.','Blood group.','Tongue rolling.','Height can take any value in a range.'),
  ('Which variation is caused only by the environment?','A scar.','Natural eye colour.','Blood group.','Scars come from injuries.'),
  ('Why does a cactus have spines?','To reduce water loss.','To photosynthesise faster.','To attract insects.','Spines have a small surface area.')],
 [('Give one example of inherited variation and one of environmental variation in humans.','Inherited: blood group / natural eye colour.~Environmental: scars / language spoken / tattoos.'),
  ('Describe how to collect and display data on the hand spans of students in your class.','Measure each hand span with a ruler in cm.~Group the data into class intervals (e.g. 16–17 cm).~Draw a histogram/bar chart of how many students are in each group.'),
  ('Explain two ways a polar bear is adapted to the Arctic.','Thick fur and a layer of fat insulate it, reducing heat loss.~White fur gives camouflage on snow for hunting.~Small ears reduce heat loss / large feet spread weight on ice.'),
  ('Explain why some features show both inherited and environmental variation, using body mass as an example.','Genes affect body build and how the body uses food.~The environment also affects mass, such as diet and exercise.~So identical twins (same genes) can have different masses.~Body mass therefore depends on both causes.')])

U('Human reproduction',
 [('Where are sperm cells made?','Testes.','Ovaries.','Uterus.','Sperm are made in the testes.'),
  ('Where does fertilisation usually happen?','Oviduct.','Uterus.','Vagina.','The egg meets a sperm in the oviduct.'),
  ('What does the placenta do?','Passes oxygen and food to the fetus and removes waste.','Makes eggs.','Makes sperm.','It is the exchange organ.')],
 [('Name two hormones involved in puberty and where each is made.','Testosterone, made in the testes.~Oestrogen, made in the ovaries.'),
  ('Describe what happens during the menstrual cycle if an egg is not fertilised.','The uterus lining thickens and an egg is released (ovulation).~If not fertilised, the lining breaks down.~It leaves the body through the vagina as a period.'),
  ('Explain how the fetus gets oxygen while in the uterus.','Oxygen in the mother’s blood passes into the placenta.~It diffuses into the fetus’s blood.~It travels along the umbilical cord to the fetus.'),
  ('Explain why pregnant women are advised not to smoke or drink alcohol.','Harmful chemicals can cross the placenta into the fetus’s blood.~Carbon monoxide from smoking reduces the oxygen the fetus receives.~This can cause low birth weight or premature birth.~Alcohol can damage the developing brain and organs.')])

U('Plant reproduction',
 [('Which part of the flower makes pollen?','Anther.','Stigma.','Ovary.','The anther is a male part.'),
  ('Which feature suggests a flower is wind-pollinated?','Feathery stigmas hanging outside the flower.','Bright petals.','Nectar.','Wind flowers do not need to attract insects.'),
  ('Which condition is NOT needed for germination?','Light.','Water.','Warmth.','Seeds use stored food.')],
 [('What is the difference between pollination and fertilisation?','Pollination is the transfer of pollen to a stigma.~Fertilisation is when the male and female nuclei join.'),
  ('Describe what happens after a pollen grain lands on a stigma until a seed forms.','A pollen tube grows down the style to the ovule.~The male nucleus travels down the tube and joins the egg nucleus.~The fertilised ovule develops into a seed.'),
  ('Describe how you could show that seeds need water to germinate.','Put seeds on damp cotton wool in one dish and dry cotton wool in another.~Keep both in the same warm place.~Compare after a few days; only the damp dish should germinate.'),
  ('Explain why it is useful for plants to disperse their seeds, and describe two methods.','If seeds land near the parent, they compete for light, water and minerals.~Dispersal spreads them so more survive.~Wind: seeds with wings or parachutes, e.g. sycamore, dandelion.~Animals: fruits eaten or hooks caught on fur / explosion: pea pods.')])

U('Acids and alkalis',
 [('A solution has a pH of 2. It is…','strongly acidic.','neutral.','alkaline.','pH 2 is well below 7.'),
  ('What colour is universal indicator in a neutral solution?','Green.','Red.','Purple.','pH 7 is green.'),
  ('Acid + alkali → ?','Salt + water.','Hydrogen + oxygen.','Carbon dioxide only.','This is neutralisation.')],
 [('Give two signs that a chemical reaction has taken place.','Colour change / fizzing.~Temperature change / solid forms.'),
  ('Name the salt made when hydrochloric acid reacts with sodium hydroxide, the other product, and the type of reaction.','Sodium chloride.~Water.~Neutralisation.'),
  ('Explain how a farmer could treat acidic soil and why.','Add lime (a base) to the soil.~It neutralises the acid.~Most crops grow better when the soil is near neutral.'),
  ('Describe how to use universal indicator to find the volume of alkali needed to neutralise 25 cm³ of acid.','Measure 25 cm³ of acid with a measuring cylinder and add universal indicator.~Add alkali a little at a time from a burette/syringe, swirling.~Stop when the indicator turns green (pH 7).~Record the volume of alkali added and repeat for reliability.')])

U('Metals and non-metals',
 [('Which is a property of most metals?','Good electrical conductor.','Brittle.','Low melting point.','Metals conduct electricity.'),
  ('Which metal does NOT react with dilute acid?','Copper.','Magnesium.','Zinc.','Copper is low in reactivity.'),
  ('The test for hydrogen is…','a lit splint gives a squeaky pop.','limewater turns milky.','a glowing splint relights.','Hydrogen burns rapidly.')],
 [('Complete the word equation “magnesium + oxygen → …” and name this type of reaction.','Magnesium oxide.~Oxidation (combustion/burning).'),
  ('Write the word equation for zinc reacting with hydrochloric acid, and describe the test for the gas made.','Zinc + hydrochloric acid → zinc chloride…~… + hydrogen.~A lit splint gives a squeaky pop.'),
  ('Explain why gold is used for electrical connections in computers even though copper is cheaper.','Gold is very unreactive.~It does not corrode or form an oxide layer.~So it keeps a good electrical connection for a long time.'),
  ('Describe how you could compare the reactivity of three metals using acid.','Use the same mass/size of each metal.~Add each to the same volume and concentration of acid at the same temperature.~Observe the rate of bubbling or measure the temperature rise.~The faster the bubbling (or bigger the temperature rise), the more reactive the metal.')])

U('Current and charge',
 [('A rubbed balloon gains electrons. It becomes…','negatively charged.','positively charged.','neutral.','Electrons are negative.'),
  ('Two positively charged objects will…','repel.','attract.','do nothing.','Like charges repel.'),
  ('Current is measured with…','an ammeter connected in series.','a voltmeter connected in series.','an ammeter connected in parallel.','Ammeters go in series.')],
 [('What is an electric current?','A flow of charge (electrons) around a circuit.~It is measured in amps (A).'),
  ('A series circuit has a cell and two lamps. One lamp breaks. Explain what happens to the other.','It goes out.~There is only one path, so the circuit is broken.~No current flows.'),
  ('Explain why you sometimes get a small shock touching a metal door handle after walking on a carpet.','Rubbing your shoes on the carpet transfers electrons, so you become charged.~When you touch the metal handle, the charge flows quickly to it.~This flow is a spark that you feel as a shock.'),
  ('Compare series and parallel circuits for the lights in a house.','In series there is one path; in parallel each light has its own branch.~In series, if one bulb breaks all go out; in parallel the others stay on.~In parallel each light can be switched on or off separately.~In parallel each light gets the full voltage so stays bright.')])

U('Potential difference and resistance',
 [('Potential difference is measured in…','volts (V).','amps (A).','ohms (Ω).','Voltage is in volts.'),
  ('A voltmeter is connected…','in parallel across a component.','in series.','anywhere in the circuit.','It measures energy difference across a part.'),
  ('Increasing the resistance in a circuit (same cells) makes the current…','smaller.','bigger.','stay the same.','More resistance opposes the flow more.')],
 [('A 9 V battery drives 3 A through a component. Calculate its resistance.','R = V ÷ I = 9 ÷ 3.~= 3 Ω.'),
  ('A 12 V supply is connected to a 6 Ω resistor. Calculate the current and state the units.','I = V ÷ R = 12 ÷ 6.~= 2…~… A (amps).'),
  ('Explain why the wires in a circuit are made of copper and covered in plastic.','Copper has low resistance, so it conducts current easily.~Plastic is an insulator.~It stops current flowing into people and prevents short circuits.'),
  ('Describe how to measure the resistance of a piece of wire.','Connect the wire in series with a cell and an ammeter.~Connect a voltmeter in parallel across the wire.~Record the current and the potential difference.~Calculate resistance = voltage ÷ current (repeat at different voltages).')])
